// src/kernel/core/cap/udp_socket_cap.pdx — R27-M6-002 / #994
// KIND_UDP_SOCKET derived capability descriptor (per-port UDP socket)
//
// =============================================================================
// DEPRECATED (R100-PREP-002, #2008, 2026-09-01)
// =============================================================================
//
// This module's `KIND_UDP_SOCKET = 0x50` is the PRE-R30-numbering-scheme
// UDP socket capability, minted through an IPC-RPC delivery path that
// was DEFERRED at R27.M6 to a "#1015" issue that never landed. The R91-
// R99 networking plan (design/networking/r91-plan.md §4.4) ratified
// abandoning that posture in favor of TCP-parallel direct-syscall
// resolution.
//
// The modern replacement is `Kind.KIND_UDP_SOCKET = 0x1A8` (see
// src/kernel/core/cap/kind.pdx and src/kernel/core/net/udp_socket.pdx),
// consumed by sys_socket(AF_INET=2, SOCK_DGRAM=2, 0) + sys_{bind,
// connect, send, recv} on a UDP socket. That path lands with R100-PREP-
// 002 (#2008).
//
// This file's mint gate remains callable but has NO REACHABLE CALLER
// in the modern tree (no syscall dispatch, no boot witness, no init
// sidecar entry seeds it). Treat `0x50` as dead weight: a stale
// reference in any dormant code must fail loudly at build time (via
// grep in a future audit round), never silently alias the new
// `Kind.KIND_UDP_SOCKET = 0x1A8` meaning. The retirement decision is
// documented in design/architecture/next-wave-derived-kinds.md's
// R100-PREP-002 entry and in Kind.KIND_UDP_SOCKET's own comment block.
//
// The file is retained rather than removed at this landing because the
// removal itself is a separate, larger sweep (audit every derived-kind
// entry the R27.M6 IPC-RPC posture touched -- the sibling AcpiCap /
// DeviceCap / BlkdevCap / InterruptCap mint-only files all share this
// deferred-since-R27 stance) that this round's scope does not include.
// A future dedicated retirement pass should remove this file wholesale
// after confirming zero reachable callers.
//
// =============================================================================
//
// Design context
// --------------
// R27.M5 landed the IPv4 substrate; R27.M6 lands the UDP header +
// parse/build + rx_handle (`net/udp.pdx`). This module gives the
// eventual userspace socket API a capability handle: KIND_UDP_SOCKET
// is minted one-per-bind and grants the holder the right to invoke
// `bind` / `sendto` / `recvfrom` / `close` RPCs against a UDP port.
//
// The design surface lives at:
//   - design/ipc/udp-socket-rpc-schema.md   — the wire format
//     userspace will speak; wire code deferred to R28+ per #1015.
//
// KIND base assignment
// --------------------
// The base-kind enum (cap/kind.pdx §Kind) is closed at 16 slots. Same
// KIND_PAGE (=4) ride as KIND_ACPI / KIND_PCI_DEV / KIND_BLKDEV /
// KIND_NOTIFICATION_CAP / KIND_INTERRUPT_CAP — the underlying
// resource is a per-port entry in a receive-queue table (backed by
// KIND_PAGE-mapped pages).
//
// Derived tag values in use across the R22..R27 rounds:
//
//   KIND_DRIVER           = 0x15
//   KIND_ACPI             = 0x20
//   KIND_PCI_DEV          = 0x30
//   KIND_INTERRUPT_CAP    = 0x40
//   KIND_NOTIFICATION_CAP = 0x41
//   KIND_BLKDEV           = 0x42
//   KIND_UDP_SOCKET       = 0x50  (this module — new range for network caps)
//
// Rights discipline
// -----------------
// Three refinement rights on a KIND_UDP_SOCKET capability:
//
//   R_UDP_BIND   (bit 0)  — bind (associate cap with a local port)
//   R_UDP_SEND   (bit 1)  — sendto
//   R_UDP_RECV   (bit 2)  — recvfrom
//
// A DNS-resolver daemon asks the broker for R_UDP_BIND |
// R_UDP_SEND | R_UDP_RECV. A monitoring probe that only speaks
// syslog outbound asks for R_UDP_SEND alone.
//
// Descriptor payload layout (24 bytes)
// ------------------------------------
//
//   +0    u16 local_port
//   +2    u16 remote_port
//   +4    u32 remote_ip
//   +8    u8  state              — 0=unbound, 1=bound, 2=connected, 3=closed
//   +9    u8  _pad0
//   +10   u16 _pad1
//   +12   u32 rights             — subset of R_UDP_ALL
//   +16   u64 _pad2              — reserved for R28+ recv-queue anchor
//
// Fixture posture at R27.M6
// -------------------------
// This module is *mint-gate only*, matching AcpiCap / DeviceCap /
// BlkdevCap / InterruptCap. The gate validates port and rights but
// the descriptor slab write was deferred until cap_revoke had a real
// body. cap_revoke now has one (R31.M1-1587, #1587: cap_revoke ->
// cap_revoke_slot, generic path for an undispatched, tail-less kind
// like this one), so that half of the blocker is resolved. The other
// half is not: a revocation must invalidate every in-flight
// sendto/recvfrom RPC citing the cap, and that logic still lives with
// the R23+ userspace-server substrate (#1015), which per R31.M1's
// #1086 reassessment has not landed a real ring-3 spawn path yet. The
// wire-up remains deferred on #1015 alone, not on cap_revoke.
//
// Reserved ports
// --------------
// Port 0 is reserved (RFC 768; some stacks read it as "assign
// ephemeral"). We reject port 0 at mint time — an ephemeral
// allocator lands with the R28+ userspace socket-server that
// consumes this cap. Port 7 (Echo) is currently intercepted by
// `udp_rx_handle` in-kernel per R27.M6 #996; a bind on port 7
// still succeeds here so a future userspace echo daemon can take
// over once #1015 lands and the kernel-side echo path is retired.
//
// paideia-as encoder-gap posture (v0.22.x)
// ----------------------------------------
// Pure arithmetic + branch. Reserved-label discipline: labels
// prefixed `udp_cap_`.

module UdpSocketCap = structure {

  // ============================================================================
  // Kind tags
  // ============================================================================
  //
  // Runtime base kind for a KIND_UDP_SOCKET capability. Rides
  // KIND_PAGE (=4) — the underlying resource is a per-port receive
  // queue backed by KIND_PAGE-mapped memory. Same posture as
  // KIND_ACPI / KIND_BLKDEV.

  pub let KIND_UDP_SOCKET_BASE : u64 = 4
  pub let KIND_UDP_SOCKET      : u64 = 0x50
  pub let KIND_UDP_SOCKET_TAG  : u64 = 0x50

  // ============================================================================
  // Rights
  // ============================================================================

  pub let R_UDP_BIND   : u64 = 0x01
  pub let R_UDP_SEND   : u64 = 0x02
  pub let R_UDP_RECV   : u64 = 0x04
  pub let R_UDP_ALL    : u64 = 0x07   // 0x01 | 0x02 | 0x04

  // ============================================================================
  // Socket state values (mirror descriptor byte @+8)
  // ============================================================================

  pub let UDP_SOCK_UNBOUND   : u64 = 0
  pub let UDP_SOCK_BOUND     : u64 = 1
  pub let UDP_SOCK_CONNECTED : u64 = 2
  pub let UDP_SOCK_CLOSED    : u64 = 3

  // ============================================================================
  // Mint return codes
  // ============================================================================

  pub let UDP_CAP_MINT_OK          : u64 = 0
  pub let UDP_CAP_MINT_BAD_RIGHTS  : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFF
  pub let UDP_CAP_MINT_BAD_PORT    : u64 = 0xFFFFFFFE

  // ============================================================================
  // udp_rights_valid(rights) -> u64
  // ============================================================================
  //
  // Returns 1 iff (rights & R_UDP_ALL) == rights; else 0.

  pub let udp_rights_valid : (u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (rights: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R27-M6-002 (#994) KIND_UDP_SOCKET rights-subset validator. Returns 1 iff (rights & R_UDP_ALL) == rights; else 0. R_UDP_ALL = 0x07 (bits 0..2). Same encoder posture as AcpiCap.rights_valid / BlkdevCap.blk_rights_valid — unsafe block per paideia-as #913 (pure-fn if-else T0532). Leaf.",
      block: {
        mov rax, rdi;
        mov r10, 0x7;
        and rax, r10;
        cmp rax, rdi;
        jne udp_cap_rights_no;
        mov rax, 1;
        ret;
        udp_cap_rights_no:
          mov rax, 0;
          ret
      }
    }

  // ============================================================================
  // udp_socket_cap_mint(port, rights) -> u64
  // ============================================================================
  //
  // Gate-only mint per the AcpiCap / DeviceCap / BlkdevCap pattern.
  // Real slab_alloc + cap_mint_write was deferred until cap_revoke had
  // a real body; it has one now (R31.M1-1587, #1587). The remaining
  // blocker is #1015 (see the module header) -- the RPC-invalidation
  // half of revoke, not cap_revoke itself.
  //
  // SysV:
  //   rdi = port (u16 semantics; must be != 0)
  //   rsi = rights (u32 semantics; subset of R_UDP_ALL)
  //
  // Returns rax = UDP_CAP_MINT_OK on success, or UDP_CAP_MINT_BAD_*.
  //
  // Return codes:
  //   UDP_CAP_MINT_OK           = 0
  //   UDP_CAP_MINT_BAD_PORT     = 0xFFFFFFFE   (port == 0)
  //   UDP_CAP_MINT_BAD_RIGHTS   = 0xFFFFFFFF   (rights outside R_UDP_ALL)

  pub let udp_socket_cap_mint : (u64, u64) -> u64 !{} @{} =
    fn (port: u64, rights: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {}, capabilities: {},
      justification: "R27-M6-002 (#994) KIND_UDP_SOCKET mint gate. Sequence: port != 0, then udp_rights_valid(rights). On any failure return the corresponding UDP_CAP_MINT_BAD_*. Real slab_alloc + cap_mint_write was deferred until cap_revoke had a real body; cap_revoke now has one (R31.M1-1587, #1587). The wire-up stays deferred so a KIND_UDP_SOCKET revoke-->EPERM-on-RPC fixture can round-trip once the userspace-server substrate (#1015) lands -- that is now the sole remaining blocker, not cap_revoke. Same structural posture as BlkdevCap.blkdev_cap_mint. Preserves rsi (rights) across the udp_rights_valid call by shuffling it into rdi. Written as unsafe block per paideia-as #913.",
      block: {
        // Port != 0 (RFC 768; port 0 is 'unspecified' / ephemeral
        // allocator; the ephemeral allocator lands with the R28+
        // userspace socket-server).
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je udp_cap_mint_bad_port;

        // udp_rights_valid expects rights in rdi.
        mov rdi, rsi;
        call udp_rights_valid;
        cmp rax, 0;
        je udp_cap_mint_bad_rights;

        // OK path — return UDP_CAP_MINT_OK (0). Real descriptor write
        // deferred (see module header §Fixture posture).
        mov rax, 0;
        ret;

        udp_cap_mint_bad_port:
          mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFE;
          ret;
        udp_cap_mint_bad_rights:
          mov rax, 0xFFFFFFFF;
          ret
      }
    }
}
